We need h such that h \equiv h^2 \pmod{24}, i.e., h(h-1) \equiv 0 \pmod{24}. Factor 24 = 2^3\cdot 3. Thus, h(h-1) must be divisible by 8 and by 3. This is equivalent to: h \equiv 0 \text{ or } 1 \pmod{8}, and h \equiv 0 \text{ or } 1 \pmod{3}. Solving the pairs via CRT:
- (0 mod 8, 0 mod 3) -> h \equiv 0 \pmod{24}
- (0 mod 8, 1 mod 3) -> h \equiv 16 \pmod{24}
- (1 mod 8, 0 mod 3) -> h \equiv 9 \pmod{24}
- (1 mod 8, 1 mod 3) -> h \equiv 1 \pmod{24}
So possible residues are 0, 1, 9, 16 modulo 24. The least h > 6 among these is 9.

We need s \equiv s^2 \pmod{15}, i.e., s(s-1) \equiv 0 \pmod{15}. Factor 15 = 3\cdot 5. Therefore s \equiv 0 \text{ or } 1 \pmod{3}, and s \equiv 0 \text{ or } 1 \pmod{5}. Solving combinations:
- (0 mod 3, 0 mod 5) -> s \equiv 0 \pmod{15}
- (0 mod 3, 1 mod 5) -> s \equiv 6 \pmod{15}
- (1 mod 3, 0 mod 5) -> s \equiv 10 \pmod{15}
- (1 mod 3, 1 mod 5) -> s \equiv 1 \pmod{15}
Residues are 0, 1, 6, 10 modulo 15. The least s > 6 is 10.

We need t \equiv t^2 \pmod{10}, i.e., t(t-1) \equiv 0 \pmod{10}. Factor 10 = 2\cdot 5. Thus t \equiv 0 \text{ or } 1 \pmod{2}, and t \equiv 0 \text{ or } 1 \pmod{5}. Solving combinations:
- (0 mod 2, 0 mod 5) -> t \equiv 0 \pmod{10}
- (0 mod 2, 1 mod 5) -> t \equiv 6 \pmod{10}
- (1 mod 2, 0 mod 5) -> t \equiv 5 \pmod{10}
- (1 mod 2, 1 mod 5) -> t \equiv 1 \pmod{10}
Residues are 0, 1, 5, 6 modulo 10. The least t > 5 is 6.

We need k \equiv k^2 \pmod{18}, i.e., k(k-1) \equiv 0 \pmod{18}. Factor 18 = 2\cdot 3^2. Hence k \equiv 0 \text{ or } 1 \pmod{2}, and k \equiv 0 \text{ or } 1 \pmod{9}. Solving combinations:
- (0 mod 2, 0 mod 9) -> k \equiv 0 \pmod{18}
- (0 mod 2, 1 mod 9) -> k \equiv 10 \pmod{18}
- (1 mod 2, 0 mod 9) -> k \equiv 9 \pmod{18}
- (1 mod 2, 1 mod 9) -> k \equiv 1 \pmod{18}
Residues are 0, 1, 9, 10 modulo 18. The least k > 5 is 9.

We need b \equiv b^2 \pmod{20}, i.e., b(b-1) \equiv 0 \pmod{20}. Factor 20 = 2^2\cdot 5. Thus b \equiv 0 \text{ or } 1 \pmod{4}, and b \equiv 0 \text{ or } 1 \pmod{5}. Solving combinations:
- (0 mod 4, 0 mod 5) -> b \equiv 0 \pmod{20}
- (0 mod 4, 1 mod 5) -> b \equiv 16 \pmod{20}
- (1 mod 4, 0 mod 5) -> b \equiv 5 \pmod{20}
- (1 mod 4, 1 mod 5) -> b \equiv 1 \pmod{20}
Residues are 0, 1, 5, 16 modulo 20. The least b > 5 is 16.
